{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["You shall not murder — every instance of רציחה is unlawful killing everywhere. ״מות יומת הרוצח״. ״הרצחת וגם ירשת״. ״צדק ילין בה ועתה מרצחים״. But הריגה and מיתה can be unlawful, as in ״ויהרגהו דקין״, and can be judicial, as in ״והרגת את האשה״. And what is written, ״אשר ירצח את רעהו בבלי דעת״ — since it speaks of a wilful murderer, therefore it states: if this רציחה was without knowledge, he is exempt. This is the answer I gave to the heretics, and they conceded to me. And even though in their books they cite ״אני אמית ואחיה״ in Latin for לא תרצח, they were not precise."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Rashbam's point is that רציחה (retzichah) specifically denotes unlawful murder, as opposed to the broader terms הריגה (harigah) and מיתה (mitah), which can cover both unlawful and judicial killing. The heretics apparently cited the Latin Vulgate of Deuteronomy 32:39 ('Ego occidam et vivere faciam') in connection with לא תרצח; Rashbam says they failed to distinguish the precise semantic range of the Hebrew root."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:42:16.001474343Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}